# Frontend Development Standards

## Stack

- React 18 + TypeScript + Vite
- React Query for server state
- Zustand for client state (when needed)
- React Router v6
- Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
- React Hook Form + Zod for forms
- i18next for internationalization scaffolding

## Component-Driven UI Architecture

- Functional components always
- One component per file
- No inline component definitions inside pages, route files, parent
  components, hooks, or render branches.
- Treat UI as Component-Driven UI Architecture: a component library built from
  small primitives, composed components, feature components, and pages. Pages
  and routes are composition surfaces: they group components for a use case and
  wire data, routing, and permissions.
- Every visible UI unit added by a ticket must have its own named JSX tag and
  component file. Do not hide new screen elements as inline JSX blocks inside a
  page or parent component.
- If the required component does not exist, create it for the page or feature.
  If it can plausibly be reused in another screen, parameterize it instead of
  hard-coding page-specific behavior.
- New components must be parameterized with typed props, `children`, variants,
  state props, or callbacks when that makes the component reusable without
  leaking page-specific behavior.
- Prefer extracting reusable or future-reusable UI into a small named component
  over duplicating markup or styling in place.
- Props typed with TypeScript (never `any`)
- Separate logic into custom hooks
- Pure components when possible
- Co-locate tests with components
- When the repository has Storybook or an equivalent component catalog, every
  new visible component must include a story covering the relevant variants and
  states. If no component catalog exists, record the visual/manual validation
  fallback in the implementation plan.

## Design System

- If the consuming repository has a root `DESIGN.md`, treat it as the
  project-local visual identity contract.
- If `DESIGN.md` is absent, inspect local Storybook, token files, Tailwind
  config, shadcn theme, UI docs, ADRs, screenshots, and existing components
  before choosing visual styles.
- If those local design sources exist, treat them as input for root
  `DESIGN.md` standardization. A frontend plan may use them temporarily, but it
  must record the missing `DESIGN.md` as a design-standardization gap unless
  creating it is already in scope.
- Do not invent a new palette, typography scale, spacing scale, radius scale,
  or component variant when project-local tokens or components already exist.
- For UI tickets, implementation plans must record a `Design System Contract`
  in `Execution Notes for Implementer`, including source, impacted
  tokens/components, planned impact, and visual validation evidence.
- Any intentional design-system change must be explicitly scoped and mapped to
  an acceptance criterion, related work item, or documented risk.
- Follow `design-system-standards.mdc` for DESIGN.md usage, fallback behavior,
  and validation expectations.

## State Ownership & Data Flow

- Keep state at the closest owner that needs to read or mutate it. The owner is
  usually the nearest common ancestor of the components that use the value.
- Use typed props and callbacks for local parent-child composition, especially
  across one or two component boundaries.
- Do not prop-drill through passive parents. If state must cross three or more
  component boundaries, reach non-adjacent siblings, or be shared by multiple
  feature areas, introduce a feature-level context, custom hook, or client store.
- Atomic and presentational components should be controlled by props and events.
  They should not import route hooks, API clients, React Query hooks, or global
  stores unless they are intentionally documented as connected feature
  components.
- Use React Query for server state, caching, loading, error, and refetch flows.
  Do not duplicate server state in Zustand or local component state except for
  short-lived derived UI state.
- Use React Hook Form, Zod, or local form state for form ownership. Promote form
  data to feature state only for multi-step wizards, persisted drafts, or
  cross-screen flows.
- Use URL/search params for state that must be shareable, bookmarkable, or
  browser-navigation aware, such as filters, selected tabs, cursors, or sort
  options.
- Use Zustand or another existing project store only for real client state that
  crosses screens, modules, or non-adjacent feature trees. Do not introduce
  Redux unless the consuming repository already uses it or a ticket explicitly
  scopes that architecture change.

## API Contract

- If the consuming repository has API contract artifacts under
  `docs/contracts/`, treat those files as the project-local API source of truth
  for frontend planning and implementation. Legacy copied artifacts such as
  `docs/api-contract.md`, `contracts/api/`, or
  `contracts/api/api-contract-source.json` should also be consumed when present.
- Use `consume-api-contract` before adding or changing API clients, server
  state hooks, mocked backend flows, forms, permissions, or capability-gated
  UI.
- Do not invent endpoints, payload fields, status codes, auth rules, feature
  flags, or response shapes. Missing behavior is a contract gap or blocker.
- Generated API contract files in a UI repository are read-only; update the API
  source repository and rerun `api-contract-sync` when the contract must
  change.

## Performance

- Initial bundle < 250KB
- Lazy loading for routes
- Core Web Vitals in green
- Optimized images

## Testing

- Vitest + React Testing Library
- Test behavior, not implementation details
- Minimum coverage: 80%
- No testing internal implementation details

## Accessibility

- WCAG AA minimum
- Semantic HTML always
- `aria-labels` where needed
- Keyboard navigation functional
- Color contrast compliant
- Focus, hover, active, disabled, loading, empty, error, and permission states
  are covered when relevant to the acceptance criteria

## Package Manager

- PNPM always (not npm or yarn)
